WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.750
Book 13 · Home to Ithaca

2
00:00:03.100 --> 00:00:11.700
<v Narrator><i>Odysseus finishes his tale and the Phaeacians sit in silence.</i>

3
00:00:11.750 --> 00:00:28.270
<v Alcinous>Bring out one more chest, and let every lord of Phaeacia add a tripod and a cauldron to it. Then set the feast, for our guest sails at sundown and he must leave us glad.

4
00:00:28.320 --> 00:00:39.280
<v Narrator><i>At sunset Odysseus boards the convoy ship and lies on a prepared bed in the stern.</i>

5
00:00:39.330 --> 00:00:46.290
<v Narrator><i>The crew carries the sleeping man and his treasure onto the shore.</i>

6
00:00:46.767 --> 00:00:55.247
<v Narrator><i>The shepherd tells Odysseus you is in Ithaca and the hero suppresses your joy.</i>

7
00:00:55.297 --> 00:01:19.737
<v Athena>Cunning, insatiable trickster, even in your own land you will not lay your lies aside! Enough now: I am Athena, daughter of Zeus, and we two are alike, crooked-minded both, you the best of mortals at scheming and I of gods.

8
00:01:19.787 --> 00:01:41.907
<v Odysseus>Hard goddess, where were you all the salt years since Troy burned? You stood at my shoulder then, and after, I never once saw you on my deck while I suffered. Now you come smiling and tell me this is Ithaca, so swear it is not one more mockery.

9
00:01:41.957 --> 00:02:00.357
<v Odysseus>So that is the shape of it. Poseidon's wrath made open rescue too costly, and you wore silence the way I wear rags. You hid me even from my own eyes to keep me breathing.

10
00:02:00.083 --> 00:02:02.833
Book 14 · Eumaeus the Swineherd

11
00:02:03.183 --> 00:02:10.143
<v Narrator><i>Odysseus approaches Eumaeus's remote pig farm in disguise.</i>

12
00:02:10.193 --> 00:02:19.113
<v Narrator><i>Eumaeus runs out, scatters the dogs with stones, and brings the stranger inside.</i>

13
00:02:19.163 --> 00:02:27.563
<v Narrator><i>Eumaeus offers his own seat and prepares two young pigs for a meal.</i>

14
00:02:27.250 --> 00:02:30.000
Book 15 · Telemachus Comes Home

15
00:02:30.350 --> 00:02:39.830
<v Narrator><i>As the ship approaches Ithaca, a hawk tears a dove beside Telemachus.</i>

16
00:02:39.880 --> 00:02:49.560
<v Narrator><i>Theoclymenus reads the omen as proof that Odysseus's line will remain supreme.</i>

17
00:02:49.610 --> 00:03:03.290
<v Telemachus>Crew, take her round to the town harbor without me; I go inland to look over my farms. Tonight I lodge with the herdsmen, and tomorrow evening I'll set out a feast to pay you for the voyage.

18
00:03:03.340 --> 00:03:13.460
<v Narrator><i>Telemachus entrusts Theoclymenus to Piraeus and walks inland alone.</i>

19
00:03:13.167 --> 00:03:15.917
Book 16 · Father and Son

20
00:03:16.267 --> 00:03:26.747
<v Narrator><i>Athena restores Odysseus's strength, clothing, skin, and dark hair outside the hut.</i>

21
00:03:26.797 --> 00:03:32.757
<v Narrator><i>Athena reenters transformed.</i>

22
00:03:32.807 --> 00:03:39.687
<v Narrator><i>Telemachus recoils, believing he is a god.</i>

23
00:03:39.737 --> 00:03:45.497
<v Odysseus>I am no god. Why do you liken me to the immortals? I am your father, Telemachus, the man you have grieved for all these years.

24
00:03:45.547 --> 00:03:49.627
<v Telemachus>You are not my father. No mortal man makes himself young at will, and no beggar puts on a king in one breath. This is a god's work, and it is mocking my grief.

25
00:03:49.677 --> 00:03:58.077
<v Narrator><i>Telemachus embrace and weep with the force of birds robbed of their young.</i>

26
00:03:57.750 --> 00:04:00.500
Book 17 · The Beggar at the Gate

27
00:04:00.850 --> 00:04:11.050
<v Narrator><i>Near the palace Odysseus sees his old dog Argos lying neglected on dung.</i>

28
00:04:11.100 --> 00:04:20.140
<v Narrator><i>Odysseus turns aside to hide a tear while asking Eumaeus about the animal.</i>

29
00:04:20.190 --> 00:04:31.990
<v Narrator><i>After twenty years Argos dies as soon as he has recognized Odysseus.</i>

30
00:04:32.433 --> 00:04:51.033
<v Odysseus>I had a house and men of my own once, and I gave to any beggar who came. Then Egypt swallowed it. My crew were cut down along the river and I was sold from hand to hand until I came to this door.

31
00:04:51.083 --> 00:05:02.083
<v Antinous>What god dumped this plague on our dinner? Back from the table, you shameless thing, before you find an Egypt worse than the first.

32
00:05:02.133 --> 00:05:17.933
<v Odysseus as beggar>Your body is handsome, but there is no mind inside it. Sitting in another man's house you would not give a grain of salt from a table that is not yours.

33
00:05:17.983 --> 00:05:23.743
<v Narrator><i>Antinous hurls a footstool into his shoulder.</i>

34
00:05:23.793 --> 00:05:37.233
<v Odysseus>Hear me, gods, and you avengers of the poor, if a beggar has any claim on heaven. Let Antinous meet his death before his wedding day.

35
00:05:37.283 --> 00:05:43.923
<v Narrator><i>Even the other suitors condemn the violation of a stranger.</i>

36
00:05:43.583 --> 00:05:46.333
Book 18 · Irus and the Suitors

37
00:05:46.683 --> 00:05:50.803
<v Odysseus>Look, then. The rags come up, and you may all see what the sea left me.

38
00:05:50.853 --> 00:05:55.693
<v Narrator><i>Athena enlarges his appearance.</i>

39
00:05:55.743 --> 00:06:02.463
<v Narrator><i>Irus trembles but is forced into the ring.</i>

40
00:06:02.513 --> 00:06:13.153
<v Narrator><i>Odysseus chooses not to kill him and breaks his jaw with one compact blow.</i>

41
00:06:13.203 --> 00:06:25.843
<v Odysseus>Sit there in the dirt and keep the dogs and the pigs off yourself. You were king of the beggars an hour ago. Come back to this gate and you will not walk away from it.

42
00:06:25.893 --> 00:06:33.693
<v Narrator><i>Cheer and Amphinomus gives the victor food and wine.</i>

43
00:06:33.417 --> 00:06:36.167
Book 19 · Penelope and the Stranger

44
00:06:36.517 --> 00:06:47.317
<v Narrator><i>Odysseus invents the Cretan name Aethon and claims to have hosted Odysseus before Troy.</i>

45
00:06:47.367 --> 00:06:59.567
<v Narrator><i>Odysseus describes the king's purple cloak, golden brooch, tunic, and herald Eurybates in exact detail.</i>

46
00:06:59.617 --> 00:07:12.937
<v Narrator><i>Penelope weeps as if melting snow while Odysseus keeps his eyes hard despite inward grief.</i>

47
00:07:12.987 --> 00:07:17.747
<v Penelope>Stranger, you were pitied in this house an hour ago. Now you will be loved in it and honored. Those were my gifts, folded by me, and no living man could have known them who had not stood beside him.

48
00:07:17.797 --> 00:07:26.917
<v Penelope>You swear to me he is near, and comes before this moon is dead. Then hear my own oath, stranger. If he crosses that threshold, no one alive will ever call you beggar again.

49
00:07:27.350 --> 00:07:44.870
<v Penelope>Eurycleia, rise and wash the feet of your master's age-mate. By now the hands and feet of Odysseus must be worn as this man's are, if he is still under the sun.

50
00:07:44.920 --> 00:07:53.160
<v Narrator><i>Odysseus recognizes the danger but cannot refuse without suspicion.</i>

51
00:07:53.210 --> 00:08:02.570
<v Narrator><i>Odysseus drops the foot into the basin, spilling water, and reaches toward Penelope.</i>

52
00:08:02.620 --> 00:08:11.660
<v Narrator><i>Odysseus grips her throat gently but firmly and makes her swear silence.</i>

53
00:08:11.333 --> 00:08:14.083
Book 20 · Omens

54
00:08:14.433 --> 00:08:23.708
<v Ctesippus>The stranger has had his share like the rest of us. Here is a guest-gift from me, worthy of the bath-woman and the servants he keeps company with.

55
00:08:23.758 --> 00:08:27.398
<v Narrator><i>Odysseus dodges.</i>

56
00:08:27.448 --> 00:08:40.989
<v Telemachus>Ctesippus, be glad you missed. If you had struck my guest, my father's spear would be in your belly, and your father would be building your tomb instead of your wedding. Not one more insult in this house. I am a boy no longer, and I see everything you do.

57
00:08:41.433 --> 00:08:51.593
<v Narrator><i>Athena drives the suitors into uncontrollable laughter while their meat drips blood in their vision.</i>

58
00:08:51.643 --> 00:09:03.523
<v Narrator><i>Theoclymenus sees darkness covering them, ghosts crowding the court, and blood on the walls.</i>

59
00:09:03.573 --> 00:09:19.973
<v Theoclymenus>Your heads are wrapped in darkness and your knees are gone. The walls are running with blood, the porch is full of ghosts going down to the dark, and the sun has died out of the sky. Laugh. I am going out of this house while its door still opens.

60
00:09:20.023 --> 00:09:28.743
<v Narrator><i>Telemachus and Odysseus watch one another across the doomed feast.</i>

61
00:09:28.417 --> 00:09:31.167
Book 21 · The Bow

62
00:09:31.517 --> 00:09:52.077
<v Leiodes>I cannot bend it. Let another try. But I tell you, this bow will take the life and the breath out of the best men here, and better to die than live on failing at what we came for.

63
00:09:52.127 --> 00:10:00.367
<v Narrator><i>One after another the men strain, sweat, and fail.</i>

64
00:10:00.417 --> 00:10:09.977
<v Narrator><i>Melanthius lights a fire and brings fat so the bow can be warmed and greased.</i>

65
00:10:10.027 --> 00:10:17.227
<v Narrator><i>Even Eurymachus cannot string it and suffers humiliation.</i>

66
00:10:17.277 --> 00:10:20.792
<v Eurymachus>It is not the marriage. It is the shame.

67
00:10:20.842 --> 00:10:28.682
<v Narrator><i>Antinous postpones the contest for Apollo's festival feast.</i>

68
00:10:29.183 --> 00:10:38.623
<v Narrator><i>Odysseus strings the bow effortlessly, as a musician strings a lyre.</i>

69
00:10:38.673 --> 00:10:42.873
<v Odysseus>Hear that. It still sings.

70
00:10:42.923 --> 00:10:50.603
<v Zeus>It is time, son of Laertes. I have set my sign on the day.

71
00:10:50.653 --> 00:11:00.933
<v Narrator><i>Telemachus places sword and spear beside him and moves to his father's side.</i>

72
00:11:00.983 --> 00:11:10.063
<v Narrator><i>Odysseus nocks an arrow and shoots cleanly through all twelve axes.</i>

73
00:11:10.113 --> 00:11:19.833
<v Odysseus>Telemachus, the stranger sitting in your hall has not disgraced you. I did not miss the mark, and the bow did not wear me out. Now it is the hour for supper, while there is still light, and after supper there is other sport in this house.

74
00:11:19.500 --> 00:11:22.250
Book 22 · The Slaughter

75
00:11:22.600 --> 00:11:34.000
<v Narrator><i>Odysseus strips off his rags, leaps onto the threshold, and pours the remaining arrows before him.</i>

76
00:11:34.050 --> 00:11:41.610
<v Narrator><i>Odysseus shoots Antinous through the throat while the suitor lifts a cup.</i>

77
00:11:41.660 --> 00:11:51.220
<v Narrator><i>Antinous falls across the table, spilling food and wine as the room erupts.</i>

78
00:11:51.270 --> 00:12:00.070
<v Narrator><i>The suitors assume an accident and search for weapons that are no longer there.</i>

79
00:12:00.120 --> 00:12:14.040
<v Odysseus>You dogs. You never thought I would come home from Troy. You ate my house, you forced my women, you courted my wife while I still lived.

80
00:12:14.517 --> 00:12:25.557
<v Narrator><i>Eurymachus blames Antinous and offers repayment many times over if Odysseus spares them.</i>

81
00:12:25.607 --> 00:12:31.407
<v Odysseus>Eurymachus, if you gave me all your fathers' wealth, my hands would not stop. Fight or run. Not one of you goes out of this door alive.

82
00:12:31.457 --> 00:12:40.097
<v Narrator><i>Eurymachus rallies the suitors to rush the archer beneath raised tables.</i>

83
00:12:40.147 --> 00:12:45.867
<v Narrator><i>Odysseus shoots Eurymachus in the chest.</i>

84
00:12:45.917 --> 00:12:53.317
<v Narrator><i>Amphinomus charges and Telemachus kills him with a spear.</i>

85
00:12:53.767 --> 00:13:04.927
<v Narrator><i>Odysseus and his allies pursue the suitors through the hall like falcons among smaller birds.</i>

86
00:13:04.977 --> 00:13:16.457
<v Narrator><i>Leiodes clasps Odysseus's knees but is killed despite claiming innocence.</i>

87
00:13:16.507 --> 00:13:27.947
<v Narrator><i>Phemius and Medon appeal for mercy and Telemachus confirms their limited complicity.</i>

88
00:13:27.997 --> 00:13:31.317
<v Odysseus>Live, both of you. My son has spoken for you. Go out to the courtyard and sit by the altar until this work is finished.

89
00:13:31.367 --> 00:13:38.407
<v Narrator><i>The living search the blood-filled hall for any remaining threat.</i>

90
00:13:38.083 --> 00:13:40.833
Book 23 · The Bed

91
00:13:41.183 --> 00:13:46.303
<v Penelope>Eurycleia, carry the great bed out of the bridal chamber and set it up in the hall. Spread the fleeces and the bright blankets on it for him there.

92
00:13:46.353 --> 00:13:59.513
<v Narrator><i>Odysseus erupts, explaining that he built the bed around a living olive tree and no one could move it intact.</i>

93
00:13:59.563 --> 00:14:05.123
<v Narrator><i>The secret proves identity.</i>

94
00:14:05.173 --> 00:14:10.853
<v Narrator><i>Penelope runs to him and embraces him.</i>

95
00:14:10.903 --> 00:14:15.703
<v Penelope>Do not be angry with me; of all men you were always the shrewdest. I was afraid some stranger would come with a smooth story and take me with it. Now the sign has come and my heart gives way, and you are as welcome to me as dry land to swimmers.

96
00:14:15.753 --> 00:14:25.913
<v Narrator><i>Athena holds back dawn while husband and wife weep and reunite.</i>

97
00:14:25.583 --> 00:14:28.333
Book 24 · Laertes and the Peace

98
00:14:28.683 --> 00:14:40.243
<v Narrator><i>Odysseus reaches the farm and finds Laertes alone in patched clothes, tending a tree.</i>

99
00:14:40.293 --> 00:14:50.693
<v Narrator><i>Odysseus sends his companions to prepare food and approaches under another false identity.</i>

100
00:14:50.743 --> 00:15:00.143
<v Narrator><i>Odysseus claims to have hosted Odysseus years earlier and watches his father collapse in grief.</i>

101
00:15:00.193 --> 00:15:07.513
<v Odysseus>Father, put down the dust. I am the man you are grieving. I am Odysseus, home in the twentieth year, and the suitors lie dead in my hall.

102
00:15:07.563 --> 00:15:11.843
<v Laertes>If you are truly my son, give me a sign I cannot argue with. Grief has made me easy to fool, and I will not be fooled a second time. Tell me something no stranger on earth could know.

103
00:15:12.350 --> 00:15:20.150
<v Narrator><i>Laertes is bathed and anointed inside the farmhouse.</i>

104
00:15:20.200 --> 00:15:29.480
<v Narrator><i>Athena makes him taller and stronger, restoring royal presence.</i>

105
00:15:29.530 --> 00:15:39.250
<v Narrator><i>Dolius and his sons return from the fields, recognize Odysseus, and join the feast.</i>

106
00:15:39.300 --> 00:15:47.660
<v Narrator><i>The reunited household eats while rumors of the massacre spread through town.</i>
